\mathcal{B}(\overline{B_s^0} \rightarrow \chi_{c1} K^+ K^-)} = (17.1 \pm 3.1
\pm 0.4 \pm 0.9)\%$. In this result, the first uncertainty is statistical, the second is systematic, and the third arises from uncertainties in the branching fractions of radiative $\chi_c$ decays. Furthermore, the $\overline{B_s^0} \rightarrow \chi_{c1} K^+ K^- $ decay mode enables the measurement of the $B_s^0$ mass as $m(B_s^0) = 5366.83 \pm 0.25 \pm 0.27 \, \textrm{MeV}/c^2$, where the initial uncertainty is statistical and the subsequent one is systematic. This finding is subsequently combined with other $B_s^0$ mass measurements obtained by LHCb.

This study examines the behavior of radial solutions to the Lin-Ni-Takagi problem within the ball $B_R \subset \mathbb{R}^N$ ($N \ge 3$), governed by the equation:
\begin{equation*} \left
\{ \begin{aligned} - \triangle u_p + u_p & = |u_p|^{p-2}u_p & \textrm{ in }
B_R, \\ \partial_\nu u_p & = 0 & \textrm{ on } \partial B_R, \end{aligned}
\right. \end{equation*}
specifically when the exponent $p$ approaches the first critical Sobolev exponent $2^* = \frac{2N}{N-2}$. We provide a comprehensive classification of finite-energy, smooth, radial solutions that exhibit blow-up. Our analysis delineates the conditions that inhibit blow-up as $p \to 2^*$, identifies the necessary criteria for blow-up to happen, and proves their sharpness through the construction of specific blowing-up sequences. Our methodology accommodates asymptotically supercritical values of $p$. Notably, we demonstrate that for $p \geq 2^\ast$, finite-energy radial solutions form a precompact set in $C^2(\bar{B_R})$ whenever $N \geq 7$. Furthermore, sufficient conditions are established for smaller dimensions in the case where $p=2^\ast$. Lastly, we compare and contextualize our findings against the bifurcation analysis presented by Bonheure, Grumiau, and Troestler in Nonlinear Anal. 147 (2016).

