This paper investigates a specific class of stochastic partial differential equations (SPDEs) defined by the following system:
\begin{equation*}
\left\{\begin{aligned}\mathrm{d}
\mathbf{X}(t)&=\mathrm{A}(t,\mathbf{X}(t))\mathrm{d}
t+\mathrm{B}(t,\mathbf{X}(t))\mathrm{d}\mathrm{W}(t)+\int_{\mathrm{Z}}\gamma(t,\mathbf{X}(t-),z)\widetilde{\pi}(\mathrm{d}
t,\mathrm{d} z),\; t\in[0,T],\\ \mathbf{X}(0)&=\boldsymbol{x} \in
\mathbb{H},\end{aligned} \right.\end{equation*}
where the coefficients are fully locally monotone within a Gelfand triplet $\mathbb{V}\subset \mathbb{H}\subset\mathbb{V}^*$. The mappings \begin{align*}
\mathrm{A}:[0,T]\times \mathbb{V}\to\mathbb{V}^*,\quad \mathrm{B}:[0,T]\times
\mathbb{V}\to\mathrm{L}_2(\mathbb{U},\mathbb{H}), \quad
\gamma:[0,T]\times\mathbb{V}\times\mathrm{Z}\to\mathbb{H}, \end{align*}
are measurable; $\mathrm{L}_2(\mathbb{U},\mathbb{H})$ denotes the space of Hilbert-Schmidt operators from $\mathbb{U}$ to $\mathbb{H}$, $\mathrm{W}$ represents a $\mathbb{U}$-cylindrical Wiener process, and $\widetilde{\pi}$ is a compensated, time-homogeneous Poisson random measure. This framework encompasses a broad range of quasilinear SPDEs and numerous fluid dynamic models. Assuming certain generic conditions on $\mathrm{A}$, $\mathrm{B}$, and $\gamma$, we employ the classical Faedo-Galerkin technique, a compactness method, and a variant of Skorokhod's representation theorem to demonstrate the existence of a \emph{probabilistic weak solution} and \emph{pathwise uniqueness}. By applying the classical Yamada-Watanabe theorem, we further establish the existence of a \emph{unique probabilistic strong solution}. Finally, we permit both the diffusion coefficient $\mathrm{B}(t,\cdot)$ and the jump noise coefficient $\gamma(t,\cdot,z)$ to depend on both the $\mathbb{H}$-norm and the $\mathbb{V}$-norm, indicating that these coefficients may also rely on the solution's gradient. Consequently, we derive global solvability results.

This paper investigates multi-species nonlinear Schr\"odinger systems in $\bbr^N$ given by:
\begin{equation*}
\left\{\aligned&-\Delta
u_j+V_j(x)u_j=\mu_ju_j^3+\sum_{i=1;i\not=j}^d\beta_{i,j} u_i^2u_j\quad\text{in
}\bbr^N,
  &u_j(x)>0\quad\text{in } {\mathbb R}^N,
  &u_j(x)\to0\quad\text{as }|x|\to+\infty,\quad
j=1,2,\cdots,d,\endaligned\right.
\end{equation*}
where $N=2,3$, $\mu_j>0$ are constants, $\beta_{i,j}=\beta_{j,i}\not=0$ represent coupling parameters, $d\geq2$, and $V_j(x)$ denote potentials. Utilizing Ljapunov-Schmidt reduction techniques, we establish the existence of infinitely many nonradial positive solutions for the system under mild conditions on the potentials $V_j(x)$ and coupling parameters $\{\beta_{i,j}\}$, without imposing any symmetry requirements on the limiting case. These findings provide an affirmative resolution to the conjecture posed by Pistoia and Vaira \cite{PV22}, extend previous results found in \cite{PW13,PV22}, and uncover a {\it new phenomenon} specifically for the case where $N=2$ and $d=2$. Furthermore, our results regarding the coupling parameters $\{\beta_{i,j}\}$ are {\it almost optimal}.

This paper establishes several Liouville theorems for elliptic equations characterized by nonlocal nonlinearities and nonlocal boundary conditions:
$$ \left\{
  \begin{array}{ll}
  \displaystyle -\Delta u(y)=\intpr \frac{
F(u(x',0))}{|(x',0)-y|^{N-\alpha}}dx'g(u(y)), &y\in\R, \\ \\ \displaystyle
  \frac{\partial u}{\partial \nu}(x',0)=\intr
\frac{G(u(y))}{|(x',0)-y|^{N-\alpha}}\,dy f(u(x',0)), &(x',0)\in\partial
\mathbb R_+^N, \end{array} \right. $$
where $\mathbb R_+^N=\{x\in \mathbb
R^N:x_N>0\}$ and $f,g,F,G$ represent specific nonlinear functions. By imposing certain conditions on these functions, we demonstrate that the system admits no nontrivial positive solutions. This work extends existing Liouville results from local to nonlocal settings, utilizing the moving plane method for the proof.

